                                     wuimg
                              Frugal image viewer

wu is a minimalistic but not barebones image viewer. It aims for comfort,
speed, accurate color rendering, and format documentation/preservation.

wu is meant as a terminal companion, so launching from one is recommended.


Features
--------
* Few keybindings
* No graphical elements besides the image; all status text goes to stdout
* Displays EXIF, XMP, and format-specific metadata
* Support for embedded ICC profiles and format-specific colorspaces
  * At the moment, images are converted to sRGB for display
* Browse archives, via libarchive
* Doesn't modify the original file when rotating!
* Runs on Wayland, DRM/KMS, and X11
* Transcode into PAM or JXL


Supported formats
-----------------
More than 108 formats are supported, ranging from the common to the niche.
Here is a representative but not exhaustive listing:
* Well-known:
  * BMP and ICO
  * GIF
  * JPEG, MPO
  * PNG
  * SVG
  * TGA
  * TIFF, BigTIFF
* 21st century:
  * AVIF and HEIF
  * JPEG-XL
  * WEBP
* Legacy:
  * ILBM/PBM, color cycling included
  * MAKI and MAG
  * MSX-BASIC
  * PCX, CGA mode included
  * Yanagisawa's PIC, Pi, and PIC2
* Specialty:
  * RAW camera formats
  * Sixel
* Uncompressed:
  * DPX
  * Farbfeld
  * The whole PNM extended family (including PAM, Pfm, Xv thumbnail, and MTV)
* Video-games/Visual novels:
  * BGI/Ethornell CBG v1
  * elf's AI5 engine GP4
  * RealLive's G00 and PDT
  * PSX's TIM

Refer to `FORMATS.txt' for a full list.

As archives are supported, CBZ/CB7 are also viewable.


Prerequisites
-------------
For building you'll need
* meson
* A C and C++ compiler (both GCC and Clang work fine, no idea about others)
* python3

and at a very minimum the development files for
* libarchive
* libepoxy
* libexiv2
* libicu
* liblcms2
* libuchardet

Window backends and decoder families can be enabled or disabled independently.
The default is to compile them if the required dev files are detected, so
usually no further configuration is needed beyond having them installed.

For window backends you need:
* Wayland
  * libwayland
  * libxkbcommon
  * wayland-protocols
* DRM/KMS
  * libdrm
  * libgbm
* X11
  * libglfw3

For image decoding:
* libavif
* libflif
* libgif
* libheif
* libjbig
* libjpeg
* libopenjp2 (JPEG2000)
* libcharls (JPEG-LS)
* libjxl (needed for encoding, too)
* liblerc
* libpng
* libraw
* librsvg
* libtiff
* libwebp and libwebpdemux

To actually run the program you'll need
* Linux (it might work on other POSIX systems, I don't know)
* OpenGL 3.3
* 128MB of RAM minimum, 256MB recommended


Compiling the viewer
--------------------
Pick a good name for a build directory and run

	meson BUILDDIR

This will by default enable as many features as possible. In case you want to
explicitely disable or enable some (like for, say, fast fuzzing because you
don't trust me or the C language in general), run some of the following:

	# See available options
	meson configure BUILDDIR

	# Set features to enable/disable/auto-detect
	meson configure -D png=disabled -D maki=enabled BUILDDIR
	meson configure -D window_wayland=auto BUILDDIR

	# Disable all formats requiring external libraries
	meson configure -D disable_external=true BUILDDIR

	# Optimize for the current CPU, at the expense of probably not
	# running on a different machine.
	meson configure -D c_args=-march=native FUZZBUILD

	# Disable all decoders and window backends by default
	meson configure --auto-features disabled BUILDDIR

Once you're ready, run

	meson install -C BUILDDIR

A lone executable `wu` and a .desktop file will be installed to whatever
location meson uses as default. This is typically /usr/local, which typically
requires root privileges. To set a different location, do

	meson configure --prefix PREFIX BUILDDIR

beforehand so that it's installed to $PREFIX/bin/ from then on. A good
candidate is '~/.local' if that's on your $PATH.

That's it. Run 'wu -h' to get some help text. Its help screen is short (it
may actually fit in your screen) and contains all the information you need.


Compiling wuconv
----------------
wu is able to encode most images into PAM or JXL (`wu write FILES...`), but
uses an OpenGL and windowing context to perform rotations and color conversion.
For headless machines or where speed is more important, there's a pure-software
converter, `wuconv`

	# Creates a binary at BUILDDIR/src/wuconv
	meson compile -C BUILDDIR wuconv

Both tools feature the same options and mostly the same output, upsampling
quality and roundoff errors notwithstanding. Main differences are
* `wuconv` is faster (GPU roundtrips may not be well implemented, or may simply
   be slow, not sure which)
* `wu write` supports half-precision floating point formats, which is used in
   PHM and occasionally JXL and TIFF


Compiling the fuzzer
--------------------
Lastly, there's a fuzzer tool to test decoders with AFL++ in persistent mode.
This is used for debugging.

	# You need AFL++ installed and a dedicated build directory
	CC=afl-cc CXX=afl-c++ meson setup FUZZBUILD
	# For better performance, disable all but the format family to fuzz.
	# To test, say, BMP decoding, you need the DIB family enabled,
	# which also includes ICO and DIB.
	meson configure --auto-features disabled -D dib=enabled FUZZBUILD
	# Optimize for the current CPU
	meson configure -D c_args=-march=native FUZZBUILD
	# Enable a sanitizer to make sure it crashes
	meson configure -D b_sanitize=address FUZZBUILD

	meson compile -C FUZZBUILD wufuzz
	# Create a minimal corpus
	afl-cmin -i bmp_samples/ -o uniq/ -- wufuzz bmp
	# Fuzz
	afl-fuzz -i uniq/ -o out/ -- wufuzz bmp


Usage
-----
Three keys you need to remember: 'q' to quit, 'n' to go to the next image, and
'p' for the previous one.

Commands:
	`wu [FILE|DIR]` for viewing.
	`wu -h` for command-line usage
	`wu -k` for a list of keybindings


Configuration
-------------
All done through a 'key = value #comment' file that must be located at
"$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/wu.conf" or "$HOME/.config/wu.conf". 'key' has to be a member
of the wu_conf struct, which is defined at 'conf.h' with plenty of comments.
You should take a look at it.

Example:
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Comment                     # A comment.
magnify_under = 128           # (unsigned) int. An (unsigned) integer.
initial_size = 1280 720       # struct. Specified in order.
bg = 0xcc 0x66 0x99 0x33      # array. Specified in order.
bg_source = metadata          # enum. Specified as the value name sans prefix
no_window_decorations = true  # bool. Specified as 'true' or 'false'. Don't you
                              # try to cheat using 0 or 1.
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------


Party tricks
------------
Have a .kra|ora file, and don't want to launch your editor for a preview?

	wu a my_masterpiece.kra  # .kra are really zip files

Have a weird file from the twentieth century, and want to use it in a modern
environment?

	wu w weird.pi  # Writes to 'weird.pi.pam'

Same, but you'd rather keep it in a more compact format?

	wu w -s weird.pi | convert - weird.png   # using imagemagick, or...
	wu w -s weird.pi | pamtopng > weird.png  # using netpbm

Have a raw data dump?

	# Interpret as 640x480 8-bit RGB. Remember to quote the string!
	wu -r 'w:640 h:480 channels:3 bitdepth:8' DUMP_FILE

Have a simple uncompressed format we don't know about?

	# 1-bit monochrome. Interpret first u16 word as width, next word
	# as height, then skip 20 bytes before reading the image.
	wu -r 'endian:little w:<u16> h:<u16> c:1 b:1 skip:0x14' FILE
